            {
              "id": "11da250d-ed84-36d6-beb1-f671e3943d45",
              "name": "Weak MD5 Hash For Security",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nWeak cryptographic hash algorithm. In Python, hashlib.md5() creates an MD5 digest, which is no longer appropriate for security-sensitive uses because collision attacks are practical. If this hash is used for integrity checks, signatures, token generation, password-related logic, or any trust decision, an attacker can exploit MD5's weaknesses to forge or manipulate data.\n\nFor this sink, replace hashlib.md5() with a stronger hash such as hashlib.sha256() when a general-purpose cryptographic digest is needed. If the code is explicitly performing a non-security task such as legacy checksum calculation or content bucketing, use hashlib.md5(usedforsecurity=False) to make that intent explicit in Python environments that support it. For password storage, do not use raw hashes at all; use a dedicated password hashing function such as bcrypt, scrypt, or Argon2.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nUse a modern cryptographic primitive that matches the actual purpose of the code. For general integrity or fingerprinting in security-sensitive paths, prefer SHA-256 or stronger from hashlib. For keyed integrity, use hmac with SHA-256. For password storage, use a dedicated password hashing algorithm such as bcrypt, scrypt, or Argon2 instead of hashlib.\n\nIf MD5 is only required for a clearly non-security use case, document that decision and construct it with usedforsecurity=False where supported. Review surrounding code to ensure the digest is not reused later in authentication, signature, authorization, cache key trust, or file verification logic.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport hashlib\n\ndata = b\"example input\"\nh = hashlib.sha256()\nh.update(data)\ndigest = h.hexdigest()\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nhashlib\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)\n- [Python hashlib documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/hashlib.html)\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [RFC 6151: Updated Security Considerations for the MD5 Message-Digest and the HMAC-MD5 Algorithms](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6151)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-327",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-327"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1d03ed76-770b-3336-bb80-3299283993ee",
              "name": "Subprocess Called With Shell=True Can Lead To Command Injection",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCommand injection in process execution occurs when untrusted data is passed to a shell-interpreted command. In Python, using subprocess.Popen with shell=True causes the command string to be evaluated by the system shell, so shell metacharacters, command chaining, variable expansion, and redirection can be abused if any part of the command is influenced by external input.\n\nFor this sink, the fix is to stop invoking the shell and pass the executable and its arguments as a list with shell=False. This prevents the shell from parsing attacker-controlled content as additional commands. If only a limited set of commands or arguments is expected, validate them against an allowlist before execution and avoid constructing command strings dynamically.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not use shell=True for commands that include variable or user-controlled content. Build subprocess arguments as a list so Python passes them directly to the target program without shell parsing. Prefer absolute executable paths, restrict callable commands to a small allowlist, and validate each argument against expected formats before execution. If shell behavior is truly required, isolate it from untrusted input and use strict input validation with narrowly defined permitted values.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\n\nallowed_commands = {\"status\": [\"/usr/bin/systemctl\", \"status\", \"myservice\"]}\nuser_action = \"status\"\n\nif user_action not in allowed_commands:\n    raise ValueError(\"Invalid command\")\n\nprocess = subprocess.Popen(\n    allowed_commands[user_action],\n    stdout=subprocess.PIPE,\n    stderr=subprocess.STDOUT,\n    shell=False,\n    text=True,\n)\nstdout, _ = process.communicate()\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nsubprocess (Python standard library)\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n- [Python subprocess module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html)\n- [OWASP OS Command Injection Defense Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/OS_Command_Injection_Defense_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-78",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1d9e913f-c9ee-38b8-b1f2-e294f9bd4b79",
              "name": "Missing Function Level Access Control",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis is a missing function-level access control issue. The view processes a request, selects a trigger function, and returns content without first enforcing authentication or authorization on the endpoint.\n\nIn Python web applications, access checks should be applied before any sensitive action is performed. In the shown sink, the request can reach get_trigger(name, trigger), invoke trigger_func(user_input), and render a response even when no identity or permission check is visible. That allows unauthenticated or unauthorized callers to exercise server-side behavior that should be restricted.\n\nThe fix is to protect the view with authentication and explicit permission checks, then keep any action dispatch behind those checks. For Django-style views, use built-in decorators such as login_required and permission_required, or equivalent class-based access controls, so the request is rejected before trigger resolution, function execution, or template rendering occurs.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nRequire authentication on the view and enforce authorization for the specific action being performed. Use framework-provided decorators or middleware so access is denied before any business logic runs. Do not rely on hidden routes, client input, or indirect dispatch as a security boundary.\n\nApply least privilege by mapping users or roles to a small allowlist of permitted triggers instead of executing arbitrary functions returned from request-controlled values. Validate route parameters, fail closed when a trigger is unknown or unauthorized, and return a 403 response for forbidden actions. Review similar endpoints to ensure every sensitive function has consistent server-side access control.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_required, permission_required\nfrom django.http import HttpResponseForbidden\nfrom django.template.loader import get_template\nfrom django.utils.html import escape\n\n@login_required\n@permission_required('app.can_execute_trigger', raise_exception=True)\ndef _view(request, name, trigger):\n    user_input = _get_user_input(request)\n    trigger_func = get_trigger(name, trigger)\n\n    allowed_triggers = {\n        'safe': safe_trigger,\n    }\n\n    if trigger_func is None or trigger_func not in allowed_triggers.values():\n        return HttpResponseForbidden('Forbidden')\n\n    trigger_func(user_input)\n\n    template = get_template(f\"{name}.html\").render({}, request)\n\n    if name == 'xss' and trigger == 'raw':\n        template += '<p>XSS: ' + escape(user_input) + '</p>'\n\n    return Response(template)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nDjango\nfrom django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_required, permission_required\nfrom django.http import HttpResponseForbidden\nfrom django.template.loader import get_template\nfrom django.utils.html import escape\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-community/Broken_Access_Control)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Django Security Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Django_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [Django login_required decorator](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/auth/default/#the-login-required-decorator)\n- [Django permission_required decorator](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/auth/default/#permission-required-decorator)\n- [CWE-306: Missing Authentication for Critical Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-306",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-306"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "280408b5-0c70-3558-ad51-586dc0bf2c45",
              "name": "Vulnerable XML Parsing With Untrusted Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsafe XML parsing. In Python, parsing untrusted XML with xml.dom.pulldom.parseString can expose the application to XML-based attacks such as external entity expansion, recursive entity denial of service, and other parser abuse when attacker-controlled input is processed.\n\nThe sink shown returns pulldom.parseString(user_input) directly, so hostile XML can reach the parser without hardening. Replace the standard-library parser with the defusedxml equivalent for pulldom, or initialize defusedxml protection before any XML parsing occurs. For this call site, the safest fix is to import defusedxml.pulldom and parse the untrusted string with that hardened implementation.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not parse attacker-controlled XML with Python XML parsers that are not explicitly hardened. Prefer the matching defusedxml module for the parser you use, and ensure all XML entry points in the codebase follow the same pattern. If legacy code must keep stdlib XML imports, call defusedxml.defuse_stdlib() during process startup before any XML parser is imported or used.\n\nAlso reduce XML attack surface by rejecting XML when it is not required, enforcing payload size limits, disabling DTD processing where possible, and validating expected structure before deeper processing. Keep XML libraries up to date and avoid mixing hardened and unhardened parser APIs in the same application.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom defusedxml import pulldom\n\ndef parse_user_xml(user_input: str):\n    return pulldom.parseString(user_input)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\npip install defusedxml\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: XML External Entity (XXE) Processing](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/XML_External_Entity_(XXE)_Processing)\n- [defusedxml on PyPI](https://pypi.org/project/defusedxml/)\n- [Python Documentation: XML security](https://docs.python.org/3/library/xml.html#xml-vulnerabilities)\n- [CWE-611: Improper Restriction of XML External Entity Reference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html)\n- [CWE-776: Improper Restriction of Recursive Entity References in DTDs](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/776.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-611",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-776",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/776.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-611",
                  "CWE-776"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2c4feb15-e318-3ee5-a0a9-e096a4a70c84",
              "name": "Starting A Process With A Shell",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCommand injection in Python process execution. Using os.system(cmd) starts a shell and passes the command string to it, so any untrusted data embedded in cmd can be interpreted as shell syntax instead of plain argument data. Attackers can exploit separators, substitutions, redirects, or environment-dependent behavior to execute unintended commands.\n\nIn Python, the safest fix is to avoid shell-based execution for external processes. Replace os.system(cmd) with subprocess.run() and pass arguments as a list with shell=False, which is the default. This prevents the shell from parsing user-controlled input and keeps each argument isolated.\n\nIf the command includes variable input, validate that input against a strict allowlist before invoking the process. When shell features are truly required, redesign the flow to avoid direct user control over the command string; quoting alone is weaker than removing the shell from the execution path.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not use os.system, os.popen, or subprocess APIs with shell=True for commands influenced by external input. Invoke programs directly with subprocess.run or subprocess.Popen using a list of arguments so the shell does not interpret metacharacters. Prefer fixed executable paths and fixed option names, and validate dynamic values with an allowlist of expected characters or known-safe values. If you must support a limited set of actions, map user choices to predefined commands rather than concatenating strings. Handle errors explicitly with check=True or return-code checks, and avoid passing unsanitized data through environment variables, file names, or command options that may alter program behavior.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\n\n# Build the command as an argument list, not a shell string.\n# Validate or constrain any untrusted values before use.\ncommand = [\"/usr/bin/example\", \"--name\", user_value]\nsubprocess.run(command, check=True)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nsubprocess\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n- [Python subprocess module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html)\n- [Python shlex module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/shlex.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-78",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2fed86ac-adca-3d48-8012-53f291ab291e",
              "name": "Missing Function Level Access Control",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis is a missing function-level access control issue. The view function handles a request and executes behavior based on user-controlled inputs, but it does not enforce authentication or authorization before performing sensitive actions.\n\nIn Python web applications such as Django, every view that can trigger server-side behavior should explicitly verify who the caller is and whether that user is allowed to perform the requested action. In the shown sink, the request can reach get_trigger(name, trigger), invoke trigger_func(user_input), and render content without any access check. That means an unauthenticated or low-privileged caller may be able to invoke code paths that should be restricted.\n\nThe fix is to require authentication on the view and add a permission check before resolving or executing the trigger. Restrict the allowed trigger names to a server-defined allowlist, reject unauthorized requests early, and avoid exposing dynamic functionality directly from request parameters.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nProtect the view with Django authentication and enforce authorization using permissions, groups, or object-level checks before any sensitive action is performed. Do not rely on hidden routes, client-side controls, or the absence of links as access control.\n\nTreat request parameters that select functionality as untrusted. Map them through a small server-side allowlist and deny unknown values. Perform authorization checks as close as possible to the action being executed, especially before calling dynamically selected functions.\n\nReturn 403 for authenticated users without permission and redirect unauthenticated users to login. Review similar views for missing decorators or direct function invocation paths, and add tests that verify anonymous and low-privilege users cannot reach restricted behavior.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_required, permission_required\nfrom django.http import HttpResponse, HttpResponseBadRequest\nfrom django.template.loader import get_template\nfrom django.utils.html import escape\n\nALLOWED_TRIGGERS = {\n    \"safe\": \"safe\",\n}\n\n@login_required\n@permission_required(\"app.can_execute_trigger\", raise_exception=True)\ndef _view(request):\n    user_input = _get_user_input(request)\n    name = request.GET.get(\"name\", \"\")\n    trigger = request.GET.get(\"trigger\", \"\")\n\n    if trigger not in ALLOWED_TRIGGERS:\n        return HttpResponseBadRequest(\"Invalid trigger\")\n\n    trigger_func = get_trigger(name, ALLOWED_TRIGGERS[trigger])\n    if trigger_func is None:\n        return HttpResponseBadRequest(\"Unknown trigger\")\n\n    trigger_func(user_input)\n\n    template = get_template(f\"{name}.html\").render({}, request)\n\n    if name == \"xss\" and trigger == \"raw\":\n        template += \"<p>XSS: \" + escape(user_input) + \"</p>\"\n\n    return HttpResponse(template)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nDjango\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-community/Broken_Access_Control)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Django Security Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Django_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [Django authentication system](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/auth/default/)\n- [CWE-306: Missing Authentication for Critical Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html)\n- [CWE-862: Missing Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-306",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-862",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-306",
                  "CWE-862"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "393820c2-e77a-3b0a-babb-393ca29206ea",
              "name": "Missing Function Level Access Control",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis is a function-level access control weakness. A request handler or helper involved in processing HTTP input is reachable without enforcing authentication and authorization, which can allow unauthenticated or unauthorized users to invoke protected application behavior.\n\nIn Python web applications, access checks should be applied before reading and acting on request data. Here, the helper reads user-controlled input from request.rel_url.query without any visible guard that verifies the caller's identity or role. If the surrounding view calls this function before checking the current user, an attacker can send crafted requests directly to the endpoint and exercise functionality that should be restricted.\n\nThe fix is to enforce authentication at the route or handler boundary and perform explicit authorization before using the query parameter. Keep the input-reading helper simple, but only call it after a trusted user context has been established and access to the requested action has been approved.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nProtect the handler that uses this helper with authentication middleware or a route-level guard so every request is associated with a verified user identity. Perform authorization checks for the specific action or resource before consuming query parameters or executing business logic. Default to deny when no user context is present.\n\nDo not rely on the presence of a query parameter, hidden field, or client-supplied flag to decide whether a caller is allowed to access functionality. Enforce permissions on the server side for every entry point, including helper-driven code paths, and centralize the check so it is not accidentally skipped in another view.\n\nIf this endpoint is intended to be public, separate public behavior from privileged behavior into distinct handlers. Log denied access attempts, test protected routes with unauthenticated and low-privilege users, and review all functions that read from request.rel_url, request.query, or request.match_info to ensure access control happens first.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom aiohttp import web\n\n\ndef _get_user_input(request: web.Request) -> str:\n    return request.rel_url.query.get(\"user_input\", \"\")\n\n\ndef get_authenticated_user(request: web.Request):\n    user = request.get(\"user\")\n    if user is None:\n        raise web.HTTPUnauthorized(text=\"Authentication required\")\n    return user\n\n\ndef require_role(user: dict, role: str) -> None:\n    roles = set(user.get(\"roles\", []))\n    if role not in roles:\n        raise web.HTTPForbidden(text=\"Insufficient permissions\")\n\n\nasync def protected_view(request: web.Request) -> web.Response:\n    user = get_authenticated_user(request)\n    require_role(user, \"admin\")\n\n    user_input = _get_user_input(request)\n\n    return web.json_response({\"user_input\": user_input})\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\naiohttp\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-community/Broken_Access_Control)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-306: Missing Authentication for Critical Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html)\n- [CWE-862: Missing Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html)\n- [aiohttp Web Server Quickstart](https://docs.aiohttp.org/en/stable/web_quickstart.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-306",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-862",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-306",
                  "CWE-862"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3d4f9bb3-2a26-38a0-b83a-4866c9b12dd2",
              "name": "Missing Function Level Access Control",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nMissing function-level access control allows a view to be reached without verifying that the caller is authenticated and permitted to use it. In Python web applications, an async handler that returns a response directly can expose sensitive pages or actions if no authentication or authorization check is enforced before rendering the template.\n\nIn this sink, the view returns an HTML template immediately from the request handler. The fix is to require login before the body executes and to enforce a permission or role check appropriate for the page being served. For Django-style views, apply authentication and authorization decorators that support async views, or perform an explicit permission check and return a forbidden response when access is not allowed.\n\nAccess control must be enforced server-side for every entry point, not only in templates, routes, or client-side code. Even if the page appears harmless, rendering it without a gate can disclose internal functionality, metadata, or linked actions that should only be available to authorized users.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nRequire authentication and authorization on every protected view, not only on URLs that seem sensitive. Use Django's built-in login and permission checks, define a clear permission such as view access for the page, and fail closed by returning 403 when the user lacks access. Avoid relying on client-side hiding, template conditions, or obscured routes as a security control.\n\nPrefer a fixed template name or a strict allowlist rather than constructing template paths dynamically. Centralize access rules where practical with decorators, mixins, or middleware, and add tests that verify anonymous users are redirected and authenticated users without the required permission receive a forbidden response.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_required, permission_required\nfrom django.http import HttpResponse\nfrom django.template.loader import get_template\n\n@login_required\n@permission_required(\"yourapp.view_protected_page\", raise_exception=True)\nasync def _root(request):\n    template = get_template(\"index.html\")\n    return HttpResponse(template.render({}, request))\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nDjango>=4.2\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-community/Broken_Access_Control)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-862: Missing Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html)\n- [Django authentication and authorization](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/auth/default/)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-862",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-862"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "43440521-d319-3495-b4ba-129bc01aea86",
              "name": "Dangerous Use Of Eval In Dynamic Content",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nDynamic code execution in Python can turn untrusted input into executable program logic. Using eval() on a variable such as code allows attackers to run arbitrary Python expressions if that value is influenced by external input, which can lead to code execution, data exposure, or application compromise.\n\nIn this sink, return eval(code) executes whatever expression is contained in code and returns the result. The safest fix is to remove eval() entirely and replace it with a parser or explicit allowlist based on the expected input format. If the value is meant to represent a Python literal such as a string, number, list, or dict, use ast.literal_eval() instead, which only evaluates basic literals and rejects executable expressions.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not evaluate externally influenced strings as Python code. Replace eval() with safer parsing that matches the expected data type, such as ast.literal_eval() for Python literals, json.loads() for JSON, or explicit mapping and dispatch for supported operations. When behavior must be selected dynamically, use a fixed allowlist of permitted values and call known functions directly instead of constructing code strings.\n\nIf legacy behavior makes removal difficult, constrain inputs strictly before use, reject unexpected characters and structures, and avoid exposing builtins or application objects to dynamic evaluation. In practice, the correct remediation for this sink is to eliminate eval(code) and use a non-executing parser tailored to the intended input format.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport ast\n\ndef execute_code(code: str):\n    return ast.literal_eval(code)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nimport ast\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Code Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Code_Injection)\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)\n- [Python Documentation: eval()](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#eval)\n- [Python Documentation: ast.literal_eval](https://docs.python.org/3/library/ast.html#ast.literal_eval)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-94",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-94"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "46eff04f-ea3a-31d9-98c7-b4aab4183b34",
              "name": "Starting A Process With A Shell",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nOS command injection. In Python, calling os.system(command) passes the string to a system shell, so any untrusted data included in command can change the command that actually runs.\n\nThis is dangerous because shell metacharacters, command chaining, variable expansion, and redirection are interpreted by the shell. For this sink, the fix is to avoid shell execution entirely and invoke the target program directly with subprocess.run() using an argument list and shell=False, while validating or restricting any user-controlled values to an allowlist of expected commands or arguments.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not build shell command strings from variable input. Replace os.system with subprocess.run or subprocess.Popen using a list of arguments and the default shell=False behavior. Use fixed executable paths where possible, and validate each user-controlled argument against a strict allowlist or character policy based on the expected format. If shell behavior is absolutely required, separate trusted static command structure from untrusted data, reject dangerous input, and prefer safer native Python APIs instead of spawning system commands.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\n\n\ndef run_command(command_name: str, argument: str) -> int:\n    allowed_commands = {\n        \"list\": [\"/bin/ls\"],\n        \"whoami\": [\"/usr/bin/whoami\"],\n    }\n\n    if command_name not in allowed_commands:\n        raise ValueError(\"Unsupported command\")\n\n    if not argument.isalnum():\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid argument\")\n\n    completed = subprocess.run(\n        allowed_commands[command_name] + [argument],\n        shell=False,\n        check=False\n    )\n    return completed.returncode\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nsubprocess\nshlex\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n- [Python subprocess module](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html)\n- [Python shlex module](https://docs.python.org/3/library/shlex.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-78",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "47c44dac-29f9-3615-bc70-7019690c6759",
              "name": "Subprocess Call With Shell=True Is Dangerous",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsafe shell command execution. In Python, passing shell=True to subprocess.Popen causes the command to be interpreted by the system shell instead of being executed directly. If any part of the command is influenced by external input, shell metacharacters, variable expansion, command chaining, and redirection can change what actually runs.\n\nFor the sink shown here, the safer pattern is to invoke the target program with shell=False and pass arguments as a list rather than a single shell command string. This prevents the shell from parsing the input and significantly reduces command injection risk while preserving stdout and stderr capture through subprocess.PIPE and subprocess.STDOUT.\n\nIf the command includes user-controlled values, validate them against a strict allowlist before execution. Prefer fixed executable paths and explicit argument construction so only intended programs and parameters can be run.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not execute commands through a shell unless shell features are strictly required and all inputs are fully controlled. Build the process invocation as a sequence of arguments, use shell=False, and avoid string concatenation for command construction. When external input is involved, validate it with a strict allowlist of expected values, lengths, and formats before passing it to subprocess APIs.\n\nUse absolute executable paths where practical, keep arguments separate from the program name, and capture output without invoking a shell. If shell behavior is unavoidable, redesign the code to remove user influence over the command string or isolate the operation behind a narrowly scoped, validated wrapper.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\n\nresult = subprocess.Popen(\n    [\"/usr/bin/mycommand\", \"--option\", command],\n    stdout=subprocess.PIPE,\n    stderr=subprocess.STDOUT,\n    shell=False,\n    text=True,\n)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nsubprocess (Python standard library)\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [OWASP OS Command Injection Defense Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/OS_Command_Injection_Defense_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n- [Python subprocess documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-78",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "48147daf-7c72-341e-856d-1d9adb126066",
              "name": "Dangerous Dynamic Execution",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis finding is a dynamic code execution vulnerability. In Python, calling exec(code) executes the supplied string or code object as Python source, which allows arbitrary statements to run in the current process.\n\nIf any part of the value passed to exec is influenced by user input, configuration, files, network data, or other untrusted sources, an attacker can execute arbitrary Python code. That can lead to data theft, command execution, privilege abuse, or full application compromise. For this sink, the fix is to remove exec and replace it with explicit, allowlisted behavior or safe parsing of structured data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not execute dynamically constructed Python source with exec. Replace it with direct function calls, a dispatch table, or strict allowlisting of supported operations. When the intent is to read simple data, use safe parsers such as json.loads for JSON or ast.literal_eval only for trusted Python literal forms, not general expressions.\n\nIf dynamic behavior is unavoidable, redesign the interface so untrusted input selects from predefined operations instead of supplying code. Validate input against a fixed schema, keep execution context minimal, and avoid exposing builtins, modules, or application objects to runtime evaluation. In most application code, the correct remediation is to eliminate exec entirely.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport ast\n\nALLOWED_ACTIONS = {\n    \"ping\": lambda: \"pong\",\n    \"status\": lambda: \"ok\",\n}\n\ndef run_action(action_name: str) -> str:\n    action = ALLOWED_ACTIONS.get(action_name)\n    if action is None:\n        raise ValueError(\"Unsupported action\")\n    return action()\n\ndef parse_literal(value: str):\n    return ast.literal_eval(value)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nimport ast\nimport json\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Code Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Code_Injection)\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)\n- [Python ast module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/ast.html)\n- [Python exec() documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#exec)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-94",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-94"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4991315d-4018-3a1b-8c91-4926c6f27649",
              "name": "Direct Use Of Jinja2 May Lead To XSS Vulnerabilities",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-site scripting in server-side template rendering. This code renders a Jinja template directly and passes request data into the template context. When direct Jinja rendering is used incorrectly, HTML autoescaping may not be applied consistently, so untrusted input can be inserted into the response as executable markup or script.\n\nIn Python web applications, the safer pattern is to render HTML through Flask's template system, which enables Jinja integration with autoescaping for HTML templates. For the shown sink, replace direct template.render(...) usage with render_template(...) and pass the user input as a template variable to a .html template. Keep untrusted data as plain data in the context and avoid marking it safe unless it has been explicitly sanitized for the exact output context.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nUse Flask's render_template() with HTML template files so Jinja autoescaping is active for browser-rendered output. Pass untrusted values only as template variables, and do not concatenate them into template source or mark them with safe or Markup unless the content has been sanitized for that exact context. Keep templates in the application's templates directory with a .html extension, and rely on contextual escaping for HTML output. If user input must appear in JavaScript, URLs, or attributes, apply context-appropriate handling instead of generic HTML rendering.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom flask import Flask, render_template, request\n\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\n@app.route(\"/template-xss/jinja\")\ndef template_xss_jinja():\n    user_input = request.args.get(\"user_input\", \"\")\n    return render_template(\n        \"template_xss_jinja.html\",\n        path=\"/template-xss/jinja\",\n        user_input=user_input,\n    )\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nFlask\nJinja2\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cross Site Scripting (XSS)](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/xss/)\n- [Flask Templating](https://flask.palletsprojects.com/en/stable/templating/)\n- [Jinja Template Designer Documentation - HTML Escaping](https://jinja.palletsprojects.com/en/stable/templates/#html-escaping)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4cd1467d-b799-3d52-a087-5378ededda28",
              "name": "Missing Function Level Access Control",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nBroken function-level access control. This view returns a template response without enforcing authentication or authorization, so any caller who can reach the route may be able to access functionality that should be restricted.\n\nIn Python web applications, access control should be applied at the view boundary before processing the request. For a Django-style function view like this one, add authentication and, where appropriate, permission checks with decorators so unauthorized users are redirected or denied before the template is rendered.\n\nBecause the view dynamically selects a template name, it is especially important to ensure only intended users can reach it and that template selection is constrained to approved values. Restrict access with explicit decorators and avoid exposing internal pages through an unprotected endpoint.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nRequire authentication on every non-public view and enforce authorization based on the specific action or resource being accessed. In Django, use login_required for authenticated-only endpoints and permission_required, user_passes_test, or object-level authorization logic for restricted content. Deny by default and make public access an explicit choice.\n\nKeep access checks close to the entry point of the view so they run before any business logic or rendering. If the response depends on user-controlled values such as a template name, validate them against an allowlist and avoid exposing arbitrary internal templates. Also review URL routing to ensure sensitive views are not reachable through alternate unprotected paths.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_required, permission_required\nfrom django.http import Http404, HttpResponse\nfrom django.template.loader import get_template\n\nALLOWED_TEMPLATES = {\"home\", \"dashboard\"}\n\n@login_required\n@permission_required(\"app.view_protected_page\", raise_exception=True)\ndef _root(request, name):\n    if name not in ALLOWED_TEMPLATES:\n        raise Http404(\"Template not found\")\n    return HttpResponse(get_template(f\"{name}.html\").render({}, request))\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\ndjango\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10: Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-306: Missing Authentication for Critical Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html)\n- [CWE-862: Missing Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html)\n- [Django authentication system](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/auth/default/)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-306",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-862",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-306",
                  "CWE-862"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5d7ffd57-ee5f-303e-aefa-f0ba35aaa3ea",
              "name": "Missing Function Level Access Control",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis is a missing function-level access control issue. The view can be invoked without enforcing authentication or checking whether the caller is allowed to use the selected action, which lets unauthenticated or unauthorized users reach sensitive behavior.\n\nIn this Python view, request data controls both the selected trigger and the rendered response. Because the handler executes trigger_func(user_input) before any access check, an attacker can invoke server-side functionality directly. The same endpoint also renders content based on user-controlled values, so protecting the route early is important to prevent both unauthorized execution and exposure of unintended behavior.\n\nApply authentication at the view boundary and perform an explicit authorization check before resolving or calling any trigger. Restrict allowed trigger names to a fixed allowlist and deny access by default when the user is not permitted to use that action.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nRequire authentication on every sensitive view and enforce authorization before executing any application action. In Django, use login_required for authenticated access and add a server-side permission check based on roles, groups, or explicit permissions. Deny by default when the requested operation is not in a predefined allowlist.\n\nDo not let request parameters directly select privileged functions unless you validate them against a fixed set of permitted values and confirm the current user is allowed to invoke that specific action. Keep access checks close to the entry point of the view so unauthorized requests fail before business logic runs.\n\nAlso avoid building responses by concatenating untrusted input into HTML. Use Django template rendering with autoescaping or explicitly escape untrusted data before including it in the response.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_required\nfrom django.http import HttpResponse, HttpResponseForbidden, HttpResponseBadRequest\nfrom django.template.loader import render_to_string\nfrom django.utils.html import escape\nfrom asgiref.sync import sync_to_async\n\nALLOWED_TRIGGERS = {\n    \"safe\": {\"staff\", \"admin\"},\n    \"report\": {\"admin\"},\n}\n\n@sync_to_async\n@login_required\ndef _view(request):\n    name = request.GET.get(\"name\", \"\")\n    trigger = request.GET.get(\"trigger\", \"\")\n\n    allowed_roles = ALLOWED_TRIGGERS.get(trigger)\n    if allowed_roles is None:\n        return HttpResponseBadRequest(\"Invalid trigger\")\n\n    user_roles = set(request.user.groups.values_list(\"name\", flat=True))\n    if not (request.user.is_superuser or user_roles.intersection(allowed_roles)):\n        return HttpResponseForbidden(\"Forbidden\")\n\n    user_input = request.GET.get(\"input\", \"\")\n    trigger_func = get_trigger(name, trigger)\n    if trigger_func is None:\n        return HttpResponseBadRequest(\"Invalid action\")\n\n    trigger_func(user_input)\n\n    template_name = f\"{name}.html\"\n    content = render_to_string(template_name, {\"user_input\": user_input}, request=request)\n\n    if name == \"xss\" and trigger == \"raw\":\n        content += \"<p>XSS: \" + escape(user_input) + \"</p>\"\n\n    return HttpResponse(content)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nDjango\nasgiref\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-community/Broken_Access_Control)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-306: Missing Authentication for Critical Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html)\n- [CWE-862: Missing Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html)\n- [Django authentication system](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/auth/default/)\n- [Django login_required decorator](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/auth/default/#the-login-required-decorator)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-306",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-862",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-306",
                  "CWE-862"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "644b8010-960a-36da-9ca3-b6c5fda7a36a",
              "name": "Insecure XML Parsing With Untrusted Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsafe XML parsing of untrusted input. In Python, calling xml.etree.ElementTree.fromstring on attacker-controlled XML can expose the application to XML-based attacks such as entity expansion denial of service and other parser abuse. Standard library XML parsers are not a safe default for hostile input.\n\nFor the sink shown here, node = ET.fromstring(xml), the fix is to replace the parser with the hardened defusedxml equivalent so untrusted XML is rejected safely. If the application must continue using standard library XML modules across a larger codebase, initialize defusedxml.defuse_stdlib() early in process startup so unsafe parser behavior is disabled before any XML parsing occurs.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not parse untrusted XML with xml.etree.ElementTree, minidom, sax, or other standard parsers unless they have been explicitly hardened. Prefer defusedxml for any XML that can come from users, files, network responses, queues, or third-party systems. Apply the hardened parser consistently at every XML entry point, and enforce input size limits to reduce denial-of-service risk.\n\nIf this codebase relies broadly on standard library XML parsing and migration is not immediate, call defusedxml.defuse_stdlib() once during application startup before any XML modules are used. Also reject unnecessary XML features, avoid resolving external entities, and prefer simpler data formats such as JSON for untrusted data when possible.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom defusedxml import ElementTree as ET\n\nnode = ET.fromstring(xml)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\npip install defusedxml\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: XML External Entity Processing](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/XML_External_Entity_(XXE)_Processing)\n- [defusedxml on PyPI](https://pypi.org/project/defusedxml/)\n- [Python Documentation: XML security](https://docs.python.org/3/library/xml.html#xml-vulnerabilities)\n- [CWE-611: Improper Restriction of XML External Entity Reference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-611",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-611"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "65d8f510-285e-3c34-b079-c11a947d763c",
              "name": "Subprocess Call With Shell=True",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nOS command injection risk in process execution. In Python, using subprocess.Popen with shell=True allows shell metacharacters, command chaining, variable expansion, and redirection to be interpreted by the system shell. If any part of the command includes untrusted input, an attacker can execute unintended commands with the privileges of the running process.\n\nFor this sink, the safest fix is to avoid the shell entirely and pass the executable and each argument as a list to subprocess.Popen with shell=False. This causes Python to invoke the target program directly instead of asking a shell to parse a command string. If external input must influence arguments, validate it against a strict allowlist and reject unexpected values before building the argument list.\n\nAlso prefer fixed command paths and explicit argument construction over string concatenation. Capture output with subprocess.PIPE or subprocess.run as needed, and handle errors explicitly so the process invocation remains predictable and auditable.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not build shell command strings from variables or user-controlled data. Replace shell=True with shell=False and supply arguments as a sequence so the operating system does not invoke a shell parser. Use absolute executable paths where practical, avoid string concatenation, and keep the command structure fixed.\n\nWhen input must affect the command, validate it against a narrow allowlist of expected characters or known values. For complex cases, map user selections to predefined commands or arguments instead of passing raw input through. Log failures safely, check exit codes, and prefer higher-level Python APIs for file and process operations when a shell is not required.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\n\nuser_arg = get_user_input()\n\nif not user_arg.replace(\"-\", \"\").replace(\"_\", \"\").isalnum():\n    raise ValueError(\"invalid argument\")\n\nprocess = subprocess.Popen(\n    [\"/usr/bin/somecommand\", user_arg],\n    shell=False,\n    stdout=subprocess.PIPE,\n    stderr=subprocess.PIPE,\n    text=True,\n)\nstdout, stderr = process.communicate()\nif process.returncode != 0:\n    raise subprocess.CalledProcessError(process.returncode, process.args, output=stdout, stderr=stderr)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nsubprocess (Python standard library)\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n- [Python subprocess module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html)\n- [Python subprocess command injection prevention guidance](https://securecodingpractices.com/prevent-command-injection-python-subprocess/)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-78",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6d662a3d-95a8-3297-bd62-f0f9f10de45c",
              "name": "Insecure Deserialization Of Pickle Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsafe deserialization of untrusted data. In Python, `pickle.loads()` reconstructs arbitrary Python objects and can execute attacker-controlled behavior during deserialization, so passing user input directly into it creates a code execution risk.\n\nIn this sink, `user_input` is encoded and then handed to `pickle.loads()`. That means any untrusted caller can supply a crafted payload that triggers dangerous object creation or arbitrary command execution when the data is loaded. For externally supplied data, use a safe data format such as JSON and validate the expected structure before returning or processing it.\n\nIf this data must come from clients, replace pickle-based deserialization with `json.loads()` and enforce strict type and field checks. Reserve `pickle` only for trusted data produced and consumed entirely within a controlled boundary, and never for request parameters, form data, headers, cookies, or message payloads from untrusted sources.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not deserialize untrusted input with `pickle`, `dill`, `shelve`, `joblib`, or other Python mechanisms built on pickle semantics. Prefer `json` for untrusted data exchange, then validate types, required fields, allowed keys, length limits, and value ranges before use. If structured validation is needed across many inputs, define a schema with a validation library and reject anything that does not conform.\n\nIf legacy code still relies on pickle internally, isolate it to trusted storage and authenticated, integrity-protected channels under your control. Do not accept pickled bytes from users. Where migration is not immediate, redesign the interface so clients send plain JSON and convert it into application objects explicitly rather than reconstructing arbitrary objects from serialized data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport json\n\n\ndef load_user_input(user_input: str):\n    data = json.loads(user_input)\n    if not isinstance(data, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"Expected a JSON object\")\n\n    allowed_keys = {\"name\", \"age\"}\n    if not set(data.keys()).issubset(allowed_keys):\n        raise ValueError(\"Unexpected fields in input\")\n\n    name = data.get(\"name\")\n    age = data.get(\"age\")\n\n    if not isinstance(name, str):\n        raise ValueError(\"Field 'name' must be a string\")\n    if not isinstance(age, int):\n        raise ValueError(\"Field 'age' must be an integer\")\n\n    return {\"name\": name, \"age\": age}\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\njson\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Deserialization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Deserialization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)\n- [Python pickle — Python object serialization](https://docs.python.org/3/library/pickle.html)\n- [OWASP: Deserialization of untrusted data](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Deserialization_of_untrusted_data)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-502",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-502"
                ]
              }
