            {
              "id": "6daea7a1-439f-3307-95f6-1c0ab0164eb3",
              "name": "Weak Or Broken Hash Function Used",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nInsecure hash algorithm usage. This code creates an MD5 hash with Python's hashlib module, which is not suitable for security-sensitive purposes such as password storage, digital signatures, or integrity checks because MD5 is vulnerable to collision attacks and no longer provides strong cryptographic protection.\n\nFor this sink, replace hashlib.md5() with a stronger primitive based on the actual use case. Use hashlib.sha256() or stronger for general hashing, use hmac with SHA-256 when authenticity is required, and use a dedicated password hashing function such as hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac, bcrypt, or Argon2 for passwords. The fix should avoid MD5 entirely in new code and ensure all callers use the stronger algorithm consistently.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not use MD5 or SHA-1 for any security decision. For general-purpose cryptographic hashing in Python, prefer hashlib.sha256() or a stronger SHA-2 or SHA-3 variant. If the hash is used to verify message authenticity, use hmac.new(key, message, hashlib.sha256) instead of a plain hash. If the data being hashed is a password, do not use hashlib directly; use a password-specific KDF such as hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac with a unique salt and strong iteration count, or a dedicated library such as bcrypt or Argon2. Review stored hashes and migration paths so legacy MD5 values are upgraded safely on the next successful verification.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport hashlib\n\ndata = b\"example input\"\nh = hashlib.sha256()\nh.update(data)\ndigest = h.hexdigest()\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nhashlib\nhmac\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Password Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)\n- [Python hashlib documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/hashlib.html)\n- [Python hmac documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/hmac.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-327",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-327"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "72ba8526-883c-3042-bdd4-277c7c1f5d8c",
              "name": "Weak Or Broken Hash Function Used",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nInsecure cryptographic hash usage. This code initializes SHA-1 with hashlib.sha1(), which is no longer appropriate for security-sensitive purposes because collision attacks against SHA-1 are practical. If this hash is used for signatures, integrity checks, token generation, or any trust decision, an attacker may be able to craft different inputs that produce the same digest.\n\nIn Python, replace hashlib.sha1() with a stronger hash such as hashlib.sha256() or stronger when a general-purpose cryptographic hash is needed. If the value being hashed is a password, do not switch to plain SHA-256 alone; use a password-specific key derivation function such as hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac with a unique random salt and a high iteration count. The sink shown should therefore be updated to a modern primitive that matches the actual use case.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nUse SHA-256 or SHA-512 for general-purpose cryptographic hashing in Python, and update all related verification code so both producer and consumer use the same algorithm. If the hash protects message authenticity, prefer HMAC with a secret key via hmac.new(..., digestmod=hashlib.sha256) instead of a plain hash. If the data is a password, store a salted password hash derived with hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac, scrypt, or another dedicated password KDF rather than a fast digest. Avoid truncated migrations that keep SHA-1 for compatibility longer than necessary, and re-hash or rotate stored values in a controlled upgrade path.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport hashlib\n\nh = hashlib.sha256()\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nhashlib\nhmac\nsecrets\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Password Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)\n- [Python hashlib documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/hashlib.html)\n- [Python hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/hashlib.html#hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-327",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-327"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "79268c67-68ca-325e-863d-3a2dc123449a",
              "name": "Insecure Deserialization Of Pickle Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsafe deserialization. In Python, pickle.load() reconstructs arbitrary Python objects from a byte stream, and that process can execute attacker-controlled code during deserialization. If user_input can be influenced by an external user, calling pickle.load(user_input) allows remote code execution, data tampering, or denial of service.\n\nFor this sink, the fix is to stop using pickle for untrusted input and switch to a safe data format such as JSON. Parse the input as plain data and validate the expected structure and types before returning it. Only use pickle for trusted, integrity-protected data that never originates from users or external systems.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not deserialize untrusted data with pickle, cPickle, shelve, dill, joblib, or other pickle-based loaders. Prefer JSON for simple data interchange, and enforce strict validation of the decoded object shape, allowed fields, and value types before use. If you must handle serialized Python objects internally, restrict that path to fully trusted sources and protect the serialized content with authentication and integrity controls such as signed data and transport security. Keep the deserialization boundary narrow and never pass raw request bodies, uploaded files, cookies, or message payloads directly into pickle.load().\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport json\n\ndef load_user_data(user_input):\n    data = json.load(user_input)\n    if not isinstance(data, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"Expected a JSON object\")\n\n    allowed_keys = {\"id\", \"name\", \"email\"}\n    if not set(data.keys()).issubset(allowed_keys):\n        raise ValueError(\"Unexpected fields in input\")\n\n    if \"id\" in data and not isinstance(data[\"id\"], int):\n        raise ValueError(\"Field 'id' must be an integer\")\n    if \"name\" in data and not isinstance(data[\"name\"], str):\n        raise ValueError(\"Field 'name' must be a string\")\n    if \"email\" in data and not isinstance(data[\"email\"], str):\n        raise ValueError(\"Field 'email' must be a string\")\n\n    return data\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\njson\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Deserialization_of_untrusted_data)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)\n- [Python documentation: pickle](https://docs.python.org/3/library/pickle.html)\n- [Python documentation: json](https://docs.python.org/3/library/json.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-502",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-502"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7a57629a-aea7-36d1-b1b5-a8cdfc47f885",
              "name": "Missing Function Level Access Control",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis is a missing function-level access control issue. The async view accepts a request and executes behavior based on attacker-controlled inputs, but it does not enforce authentication or authorization before invoking application logic and returning content.\n\nIn Python web handlers, access checks must happen at the start of the request path, before sensitive actions are performed. In the shown sink, the handler reads user input, resolves a trigger function dynamically, calls it, and renders a template without verifying that the caller is logged in or allowed to use that feature. That allows unauthenticated or unauthorized users to reach code paths that should be restricted.\n\nFor this aiohttp-style handler, require an authenticated user on the request, verify the user is permitted to access the specific route or trigger, and fail closed with 401 or 403 before calling trigger_func or building the response. Keep authorization checks server-side and explicit for each protected action rather than relying on hidden routes or client behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nProtect the handler with authentication and perform authorization before any side effects, dynamic dispatch, or response generation. Deny access by default and return 401 for unauthenticated requests and 403 for authenticated users without the required permission.\n\nDo not decide access based on client-provided parameters alone. Map routes and trigger names to explicit server-side permissions or roles, and validate those permissions before calling helper functions such as get_trigger(). If multiple handlers require the same policy, enforce it with aiohttp middleware or decorators so the check is applied consistently.\n\nAvoid exposing dangerous or administrative trigger paths through a generic endpoint. Prefer a fixed allowlist of operations and keep authorization logic close to the action being performed. Log denied access attempts and review protected routes regularly to ensure newly added views also require authentication and authorization.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom aiohttp import web\nfrom functools import wraps\n\n\ndef login_required(handler):\n    @wraps(handler)\n    async def wrapper(request):\n        user = request.get(\"user\")\n        if user is None:\n            raise web.HTTPUnauthorized(text=\"Authentication required\")\n        return await handler(request)\n    return wrapper\n\n\ndef user_can_access_trigger(user, name, trigger):\n    allowed = user.get(\"allowed_triggers\", set())\n    return (name, trigger) in allowed\n\n\n@login_required\nasync def _view(request):\n    user = request[\"user\"]\n    name = request.match_info.get(\"name\", \"\")\n    trigger = request.match_info.get(\"trigger\", \"\")\n\n    if not user_can_access_trigger(user, name, trigger):\n        raise web.HTTPForbidden(text=\"Forbidden\")\n\n    user_input = _get_user_input(request)\n    trigger_func = get_trigger(name, trigger)\n\n    if trigger_func is None:\n        raise web.HTTPNotFound(text=\"Not found\")\n\n    trigger_func(user_input)\n\n    template = get_template(\"{}.html\".format(name))\n    return web.Response(text=template, content_type=\"text/html\")\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\naiohttp\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-community/Broken_Access_Control)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Authentication Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authentication_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-862: Missing Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html)\n- [aiohttp Web Server Quickstart](https://docs.aiohttp.org/en/stable/web_quickstart.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-862",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-862"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7f18b2d5-9d33-36bc-97ae-4aec5ba666e8",
              "name": "Insecure Deserialization of Untrusted Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsafe deserialization of untrusted data. In Python, calling pickle.loads on data derived from user input is dangerous because the pickle format can execute arbitrary code during object reconstruction, not just decode passive data. If an attacker controls the serialized payload, this sink can lead directly to remote code execution.\n\nIn this case, the code deserializes user_input after encoding it to bytes and passes it into pickle.loads. That does not make the operation safe; it only changes the string into the byte format expected by pickle. The fix is to stop using pickle for untrusted input and switch to a data-only format such as JSON, then validate the parsed structure and types before using the result.\n\nWhen only simple values are needed, deserialize with json.loads and enforce the expected schema explicitly. Reserve pickle for trusted, internal data only, and never accept pickled content from HTTP requests, form fields, cookies, message queues, or other attacker-controlled sources.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not use pickle.loads, pickle.load, marshal, or shelve on data that can be influenced by users. Replace unsafe deserialization with JSON or another data-only format and validate the decoded content against the exact structure your code expects. Reject unexpected keys, nested objects, and incorrect types before processing.\n\nIf legacy code still depends on pickle, isolate it to trusted data created and consumed entirely within your application boundary. Treat any serialized payload from external clients as hostile. Add input size limits, strict schema checks, and error handling so malformed input is rejected safely without exposing internal behavior.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport json\n\ndef load_user_data(user_input: str):\n    data = json.loads(user_input)\n    if not isinstance(data, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"Expected a JSON object\")\n\n    allowed_keys = {\"name\", \"age\"}\n    if not set(data.keys()).issubset(allowed_keys):\n        raise ValueError(\"Unexpected fields in input\")\n\n    name = data.get(\"name\")\n    age = data.get(\"age\")\n\n    if not isinstance(name, str):\n        raise ValueError(\"Field 'name' must be a string\")\n    if not isinstance(age, int):\n        raise ValueError(\"Field 'age' must be an integer\")\n\n    return {\"name\": name, \"age\": age}\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\njson (Python standard library)\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Deserialization_of_untrusted_data)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)\n- [Python Documentation: pickle](https://docs.python.org/3/library/pickle.html)\n- [Python Documentation: json](https://docs.python.org/3/library/json.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-502",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-502"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "879ada17-1f55-367f-9351-21fdb24acb7e",
              "name": "Untrusted Input Allows Arbitrary Code Execution",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis issue is an unsafe dynamic module loading vulnerability. In Python, passing untrusted data to importlib.import_module() allows external input to decide which module is imported and executed.\n\nWhen the sink is return import_module(module_name), an attacker who controls module_name can trigger imports of unexpected modules, reach code with dangerous import side effects, or load attacker-influenced code from the Python path. The fix is to stop importing arbitrary module names and instead map allowed user-facing values to a fixed set of known-safe modules.\n\nIf runtime selection is required, validate the input against a strict allowlist and reject anything not explicitly permitted. Keep the allowlist close to the import site, avoid constructing dotted paths from user input, and return only modules that are part of the trusted application design.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nReplace dynamic imports based on raw input with a fixed dispatch table of approved modules or handlers. Accept simple user-facing identifiers and translate them to constant module paths under application control. Do not concatenate package names, prefixes, or dotted paths from request data.\n\nWhere possible, import trusted modules statically at startup and select among already-imported objects instead of importing at request time. If plugin behavior is necessary, store plugin metadata in code or trusted configuration, verify it before use, and limit loading to internal packages. Also review sys.path and deployment packaging so untrusted locations cannot shadow approved modules.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom importlib import import_module\n\n_ALLOWED_MODULES = {\n    \"json\": \"json\",\n    \"csv\": \"csv\",\n    \"xml\": \"xml.etree.ElementTree\",\n}\n\ndef load_module(module_name: str):\n    if module_name not in _ALLOWED_MODULES:\n        raise ValueError(\"Unsupported module\")\n\n    return import_module(_ALLOWED_MODULES[module_name])\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nfrom importlib import import_module\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Unsafe Use of Reflection](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Unsafe_use_of_Reflection)\n- [CWE-470: Use of Externally-Controlled Input to Select Classes or Code](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/470.html)\n- [Python docs: importlib.import_module](https://docs.python.org/3/library/importlib.html#importlib.import_module)\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-470",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/470.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-94",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-470",
                  "CWE-94"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8f69c26e-7711-3f75-90bf-2954da075ed7",
              "name": "Insecure XML Parsing With Untrusted Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsafe XML parsing with untrusted input. In Python, parsing attacker-controlled XML with lxml.etree.parse can allow XML external entity expansion, file disclosure, server-side request forgery, or denial of service through entity expansion and deeply nested payloads.\n\nThe sink shown parses XML from a StringIO source, which means the content may come directly from untrusted input. Replace direct use of lxml.etree.parse with a hardened parser that forbids dangerous XML features. For this case, use defusedxml.lxml.parse so external entities and related XML parser attacks are blocked before the document is processed.\n\nIf the application only needs simple structured data, prefer safer formats such as JSON. When XML must be accepted, validate the document shape, keep parser features locked down, and reject unexpected DTDs, entities, or oversized input before further processing.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not parse untrusted XML with the default lxml parsing entry points. Use the defusedxml wrapper for XML parsing so dangerous entity resolution and expansion behaviors are disabled by default. Apply this consistently anywhere XML is accepted from requests, files, message queues, or upstream services.\n\nAlso enforce input size limits, schema or structural validation, and fail closed on malformed XML. If business requirements do not depend on DTDs or external entities, keep them entirely disabled. Review downstream XPath, XSLT, and serialization logic as well so untrusted XML is not reintroduced into unsafe processing paths.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom io import StringIO\nfrom defusedxml.lxml import parse\n\ndef load_xml(xml: str):\n    tree = parse(StringIO(xml))\n    return tree\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\ndefusedxml\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: XML External Entity (XXE) Processing](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/XML_External_Entity_(XXE)_Processing)\n- [OWASP XML Security Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/XML_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-611: Improper Restriction of XML External Entity Reference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html)\n- [defusedxml project documentation](https://pypi.org/project/defusedxml/)\n- [lxml parsing documentation](https://lxml.de/parsing.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-611",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-611"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "917d64cb-c673-3bf6-ba2c-e13de68da33b",
              "name": "Missing Function Level Access Control",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nMissing function-level access control. This view returns a response without enforcing authentication or authorization checks, which can allow unauthenticated or low-privileged users to reach functionality that should be restricted.\n\nIn Python web applications, access control should be applied at the view boundary before any protected content is rendered. For a handler like this one, add an authentication requirement and, when appropriate, a permission check so the request is rejected before the template is selected and returned.\n\nThe sink shown is a request handler that serves a template directly. If this endpoint is intended for internal, administrative, or user-specific content, protecting it with view decorators is the correct remediation. This ensures the framework consistently redirects unauthenticated users or returns a forbidden response for users who lack the required permission.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nRequire authentication on every non-public view and enforce authorization based on the specific action or resource being accessed. In Django, use built-in decorators such as login_required and permission_required for function-based views, or equivalent permission classes for Django REST Framework views.\n\nDo not rely on template names, routing obscurity, or client-side controls to protect sensitive endpoints. Define which views are public, which require a logged-in user, and which require explicit permissions or group membership, then enforce those rules server-side as close to the view entry point as possible.\n\nIf this endpoint is public by design, document that decision explicitly and ensure it does not expose sensitive data. Otherwise, prefer deny-by-default routing and add tests that verify unauthenticated requests are redirected or rejected and unauthorized users receive a forbidden response.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_required, permission_required\nfrom django.http import HttpResponseForbidden\nfrom rest_framework.response import Response\n\n@login_required\n@permission_required('yourapp.view_protected_page', raise_exception=True)\ndef _root(request):\n    return Response(get_template(f\"{name}.html\"))\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nDjango\ndjangorestframework\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-community/Broken_Access_Control)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [Django authentication system](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/auth/default/)\n- [Django view decorators](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/http/decorators/)\n- [CWE-306: Missing Authentication for Critical Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html)\n- [CWE-862: Missing Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-306",
