            {
              "id": "971fb3d3-2e80-39f1-a029-be7c5b5ed5ef",
              "name": "Insecure YAML Deserialization Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsafe YAML deserialization. In Python, calling PyYAML with yaml.load(..., Loader=yaml.UnsafeLoader) allows YAML tags to construct arbitrary Python objects during parsing. If an attacker can influence the YAML content, deserialization can trigger code execution and lead to file theft, malware installation, or full process compromise.\n\nThe shown sink uses yaml.UnsafeLoader directly on user-controlled input and returns the parsed result. Replace it with yaml.safe_load or yaml.load(..., Loader=yaml.SafeLoader) so only standard YAML types are accepted. If the application needs specific structured data, validate the parsed object shape after loading and reject unexpected keys or types before using the result.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not deserialize untrusted YAML with yaml.load using yaml.Loader, yaml.CLoader, yaml.UnsafeLoader, or any loader that permits arbitrary Python object construction. For untrusted input, use yaml.safe_load or yaml.load with yaml.SafeLoader. After parsing, enforce the expected schema by checking container types, required fields, value types, and size limits before the data reaches business logic. If advanced object deserialization is truly required, restrict input to trusted sources only and isolate that processing path from externally supplied data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport yaml\n\ndef parse_yaml(user_input: str):\n    data = yaml.safe_load(user_input)\n    if data is None:\n        return {}\n    if not isinstance(data, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"Expected a YAML mapping\")\n    return data\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nPyYAML\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Deserialization_of_untrusted_data)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)\n- [PyYAML Documentation](https://pyyaml.org/wiki/PyYAMLDocumentation)\n- [Unsafe PyYAML load Remote Code Execution](https://sourcery.ai/vulnerabilities/python-pyyaml-unsafe-load-rce)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-502",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-502"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a68f8a51-3c30-3334-ac76-850ef44bcdf1",
              "name": "Formatted SQL Query",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nSQL injection in database execution is present where a query string is built with formatted or concatenated input and then passed to cursor.execute(sql). In Python, this allows untrusted values to change the structure of the SQL statement, which can bypass filters, expose data, or modify records unexpectedly.\n\nThe fix is to keep SQL code static and pass user-controlled values separately as bound parameters. For this sink, call cursor.execute() with a parameterized statement and a parameter tuple or mapping supported by the database driver. Do not escape values manually and do not interpolate them with f-strings, %, or str.format().\n\nIf any dynamic part of the statement is an identifier such as a table name, column name, or sort direction, parameter binding does not apply. Restrict those values to a fixed allowlist in Python before composing the SQL, and continue to bind all data values as parameters.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nReplace formatted SQL with parameterized queries everywhere this pattern appears. Use the placeholder style required by the active Python DB driver, such as ? for sqlite3 or %s for psycopg, and pass parameters in the second argument to execute(). Validate and allowlist any non-data SQL fragments such as column names, table names, or ORDER BY direction before building the statement. Apply least-privilege database accounts so that a successful injection has limited impact, and keep query construction centralized to make unsafe string formatting easier to prevent during review.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nuser_id = request.args[\"user_id\"]\n\nsql = \"SELECT id, username, email FROM users WHERE id = ?\"\ncursor.execute(sql, (user_id,))\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nsqlite3\npsycopg[binary]\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)\n- [Python sqlite3 documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/sqlite3.html)\n- [Psycopg parameter binding documentation](https://www.psycopg.org/psycopg3/docs/basic/params.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-89",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-89"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a6c0f725-7a7c-384a-8971-38a555276301",
              "name": "Insecure Template Autoescape Configuration",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-site scripting risk from unescaped template rendering.\n\nIn Python, creating a Jinja2 Environment without enabling autoescaping can cause HTML templates to render attacker-controlled data as active markup or script. If any variable passed into the template contains malicious HTML or JavaScript, the browser may execute it instead of displaying it as text.\n\nFor the shown sink, the Environment is created with only a loader, so autoescaping remains disabled unless explicitly configured. Configure the environment with autoescape=select_autoescape(...) so HTML, XML, and similar template types are escaped by default while preserving normal behavior for non-HTML templates.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nEnable Jinja2 autoescaping for all HTML-rendering environments and prefer select_autoescape so escaping is applied based on template type. Treat all template variables as untrusted unless they are explicitly sanitized and intentionally marked safe.\n\nAvoid disabling autoescape globally or using safe rendering shortcuts for convenience. If specific content must contain trusted HTML, sanitize it first with an allowlist-based HTML sanitizer and only then mark that specific value safe instead of weakening escaping for the entire environment. Review templates for direct insertion into JavaScript, CSS, or URL contexts, because those contexts require context-appropriate encoding beyond basic HTML escaping.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom jinja2 import Environment, select_autoescape\n\nenvironment = Environment(\n    loader=loader,\n    autoescape=select_autoescape(\n        enabled_extensions=(\"html\", \"htm\", \"xml\"),\n        default_for_string=True,\n        default=False,\n    ),\n)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\njinja2\nmarkupsafe\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cross Site Scripting (XSS)](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/xss/)\n- [Jinja2 Environment API](https://jinja.palletsprojects.com/en/stable/api/#jinja2.Environment)\n- [Jinja2 select_autoescape](https://jinja.palletsprojects.com/en/stable/api/#jinja2.select_autoescape)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ab752e7e-8dec-3f8d-ad3f-2342ea5048b4",
              "name": "Insecure Function Usage - Consider Safer Alternatives",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nDynamic code evaluation. In Python, calling eval on data that can be influenced by an attacker allows arbitrary Python expressions to run in the current process. In the sink shown as return eval(code), untrusted input can execute code, access application state, or invoke dangerous built-ins.\n\nFor this case, replace eval with ast.literal_eval when the intent is to parse a Python literal such as a string, number, list, dict, tuple, boolean, or None. literal_eval rejects executable expressions and only accepts basic literal structures, which prevents code execution while preserving simple data parsing.\n\nIf the input is not meant to be a Python literal, do not evaluate it as code. Define an explicit parsing format such as JSON or map allowed commands to fixed functions, and reject anything outside that allowlist. This keeps behavior predictable and removes the code execution path at this return point.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not use eval, exec, or compile on untrusted input. For simple structured data, use ast.literal_eval for Python literals or json.loads for JSON payloads. If the application supports a limited set of operations, implement an explicit allowlist that maps approved input values to predefined behavior instead of interpreting raw strings as code. Also validate input size and type, and handle ValueError and SyntaxError so malformed input is rejected cleanly rather than executed or propagated unexpectedly.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport ast\n\ndef parse_code(code: str):\n    return ast.literal_eval(code)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nimport ast\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-95: Improper Neutralization of Directives in Dynamically Evaluated Code](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/95.html)\n- [Python Documentation: ast.literal_eval](https://docs.python.org/3/library/ast.html#ast.literal_eval)\n- [OWASP: Code Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Code_Injection)\n- [Python Documentation: eval](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#eval)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-95",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/95.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-95"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c2381e64-4652-37de-847a-7576837b5c81",
              "name": "Arbitrary Code Execution Via Eval/Exec/Compile",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nDynamic code execution from untrusted input. In Python, passing attacker-controlled text to compile() turns data into executable bytecode, which can then be run and lead to arbitrary code execution in the current process.\n\nThe sink shown uses compile(code, \"vulnpy.py\", \"exec\"), which is specifically intended for compiling statement blocks. If code can be influenced by a user, request, file, message, or database field, an attacker can inject Python statements that execute with the application's privileges.\n\nFix this by removing dynamic compilation for untrusted input. If the goal is to process structured input, use a safe parser such as json.loads() or ast.literal_eval() for Python literals only. If only a limited set of actions is needed, map allowed commands to predefined functions instead of interpreting source text.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not use compile(), eval(), or exec() on any value that is not a trusted, hardcoded program fragment. Treat incoming text as data, not source code. For configuration or message payloads, prefer JSON and validate expected keys and types before use. If you need to support a small expression or command language, implement an allowlist-based parser and dispatch only to predefined functions. If legacy behavior cannot be removed immediately, isolate execution in a strongly sandboxed separate process with minimal privileges, but the preferred fix is to eliminate dynamic code execution entirely.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport json\n\ndef run_action(payload: str):\n    data = json.loads(payload)\n\n    actions = {\n        \"ping\": lambda: {\"status\": \"ok\"},\n        \"version\": lambda: {\"version\": \"1.0\"},\n    }\n\n    action = data.get(\"action\")\n    handler = actions.get(action)\n    if handler is None:\n        raise ValueError(\"unsupported action\")\n\n    return handler()\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nimport json\nimport ast\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Code Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Code_Injection)\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)\n- [Python docs: compile()](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#compile)\n- [Python docs: ast module](https://docs.python.org/3/library/ast.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-94",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-94"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c5b49cf2-f7b0-376e-a2ff-97265467e8c3",
              "name": "Weak SHA1 Hash For Security",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nWeak cryptographic hash algorithm. In Python, creating a digest with hashlib.sha1() uses SHA-1, which is no longer appropriate for security-sensitive uses such as signatures, integrity protection against active attackers, token derivation, or password handling.\n\nSHA-1 has practical collision weaknesses, so code that relies on it for security properties can be bypassed. For the sink shown, replace hashlib.sha1() with a modern hash such as hashlib.sha256() or hashlib.sha512() when a general-purpose cryptographic digest is needed. If the code is only computing a non-security checksum for legacy compatibility, explicitly set usedforsecurity=False to document that intent where supported.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nUse SHA-256 or stronger algorithms for security-relevant hashing in Python. Reserve SHA-1 only for legacy interoperability or non-security checksum scenarios, and in those cases call hashlib.sha1(usedforsecurity=False) when the runtime supports it. For passwords, do not use a fast hash directly; use a dedicated password hashing function such as hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac, bcrypt, scrypt, or Argon2. Review any stored digests, signatures, cache keys, or protocol fields that depend on SHA-1 and migrate them to a stronger construction with a compatibility plan if existing data must still be read.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport hashlib\n\ndef compute_digest(data: bytes) -> str:\n    h = hashlib.sha256()\n    h.update(data)\n    return h.hexdigest()\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nhashlib (Python standard library)\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)\n- [Python hashlib documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/hashlib.html)\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Password Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-327",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-327"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cb17dcd5-5c8d-3474-b836-fdc0e7c4a020",
              "name": "Starting A Process With A Shell",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCommand injection in Python process execution. Using os.system(cmd) starts a shell and passes the command string to it, so any untrusted data embedded in cmd can be interpreted as shell syntax instead of plain argument data. Attackers can exploit separators, substitutions, redirects, or environment-dependent behavior to execute unintended commands.\n\nIn Python, the safest fix is to avoid shell-based execution for external processes. Replace os.system(cmd) with subprocess.run() and pass arguments as a list with shell=False, which is the default. This prevents the shell from parsing user-controlled input and keeps each argument isolated.\n\nIf the command includes variable input, validate that input against a strict allowlist before invoking the process. When shell features are truly required, redesign the flow to avoid direct user control over the command string; quoting alone is weaker than removing the shell from the execution path.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not use os.system, os.popen, or subprocess APIs with shell=True for commands influenced by external input. Invoke programs directly with subprocess.run or subprocess.Popen using a list of arguments so the shell does not interpret metacharacters. Prefer fixed executable paths and fixed option names, and validate dynamic values with an allowlist of expected characters or known-safe values. If you must support a limited set of actions, map user choices to predefined commands rather than concatenating strings. Handle errors explicitly with check=True or return-code checks, and avoid passing unsanitized data through environment variables, file names, or command options that may alter program behavior.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\n\n# Build the command as an argument list, not a shell string.\n# Validate or constrain any untrusted values before use.\ncommand = [\"/usr/bin/example\", \"--name\", user_value]\nsubprocess.run(command, check=True)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nsubprocess\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n- [Python subprocess module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html)\n- [Python shlex module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/shlex.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-78",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cedba811-ad8d-3d44-b846-d20a242f5e32",
              "name": "Arbitrary Code Execution Via Eval/Exec/Compile",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nDynamic code execution through untrusted input. In Python, calling eval on data that can be influenced by a user allows that data to be executed as Python code, which can lead to arbitrary command execution, data theft, or full application compromise.\n\nThe sink shown here, return eval(code), is especially dangerous because it directly evaluates the code variable and returns the result with no validation or restriction. The safest fix is to stop interpreting input as code. If the intent is to parse simple Python literals such as strings, numbers, lists, or dicts, replace eval with ast.literal_eval so only literal values are accepted and executable expressions are rejected.\n\nIf the input is not meant to be Python syntax at all, redesign the interface to accept structured data such as JSON or map explicit command names to allowed functions. Avoid exec, eval, and compile for runtime behavior driven by external input.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not execute user-controlled strings as Python code. Replace eval with a safe parser that matches the expected input format. For Python literal data, use ast.literal_eval. For structured payloads, use json.loads. If behavior selection is required, implement an allowlist that maps permitted names to predefined functions instead of evaluating expressions. Keep dynamic execution APIs out of request paths, CLI argument handling, message consumers, and deserialization logic unless the input is fully trusted and isolated.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport ast\n\ndef run_code(code: str):\n    return ast.literal_eval(code)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nimport ast\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Code Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Code_Injection)\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)\n- [Python Documentation: ast.literal_eval](https://docs.python.org/3/library/ast.html#ast.literal_eval)\n- [Python Documentation: eval()](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#eval)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-94",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-94"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d5153ce7-eb32-3e5a-aaa1-b6a2fa505ca3",
              "name": "Weak MD5 Hash For Security",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nWeak cryptographic hash usage. In Python, MD5 is no longer appropriate for security-sensitive hashing because it is vulnerable to collision attacks and should not be used to protect integrity, derive security decisions, or process untrusted input for security purposes.\n\nThe sink shown computes an MD5 digest from user-controlled input and hex-encodes it. Replace MD5 with a modern hash from hashlib such as SHA-256 for general-purpose security hashing. If the hash is only needed for a non-security purpose such as stable bucketing or duplicate detection, explicitly mark that intent with usedforsecurity=False where supported so the code does not imply a security guarantee it does not provide.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nUse hashlib.sha256() or a stronger modern algorithm for security-relevant hashing. Reserve MD5 only for legacy interoperability or clearly non-security use cases, and in those cases pass usedforsecurity=False when the runtime supports it. Ensure the input passed to the hash function is bytes; if the value is text, encode it explicitly with UTF-8 before hashing. For password storage, do not use fast general-purpose hashes at all; use a dedicated password hashing function such as bcrypt, scrypt, or Argon2.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom binascii import hexlify\nfrom hashlib import sha256\n\ndigest = hexlify(sha256(user_input).digest()).decode(\"utf8\")\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nfrom hashlib import sha256\nfrom binascii import hexlify\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Use of Weak Hashes](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Use_of_weak_hashes)\n- [CWE-328: Use of Weak Hash](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/328.html)\n- [Python hashlib documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/hashlib.html)\n- [Python binascii documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/binascii.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-328",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/328.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-328"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e0ac4dd1-c669-3199-ae55-55983743fef3",
              "name": "Missing Function Level Access Control",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis issue is missing function-level access control. In Python web applications, a view or helper that processes request data without enforcing authentication and authorization can allow unauthenticated users or users with insufficient privileges to reach sensitive behavior.\n\nIn the shown sink, the function reads user-controlled input directly from request.GET and request.POST. If the surrounding view can be invoked without access checks, an attacker can supply arbitrary values and exercise application functionality that should be restricted. The fix is to enforce authentication on the view entry point and add an explicit permission or role check before calling code that reads or acts on request data.\n\nFor Django, apply authentication decorators such as login_required and verify a concrete permission with permission_required or a server-side check against request.user. Keep the helper limited to parsing input, and place authorization decisions at the view or controller layer so every execution path is protected.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nRequire authentication for every view that exposes non-public functionality, and enforce authorization with explicit permissions or role checks on the server side. Do not rely on hidden form fields, client-side controls, or the presence of request parameters to decide who may access a function.\n\nApply access-control checks at the view entry point before reading or acting on request data, and use deny-by-default routing for sensitive endpoints. In Django, prefer login_required, permission_required, UserPassesTestMixin, or equivalent class-based view mixins so protection is consistent and reviewable. If this helper is reused by multiple views, ensure each caller performs the same authentication and authorization checks before invoking it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_required, permission_required\nfrom django.http import HttpResponseForbidden\n\n\ndef _get_user_input(request):\n    if request.method == \"GET\":\n        return request.GET.get(\"user_input\", \"\")\n    return request.POST.get(\"user_input\", \"\")\n\n\n@login_required\n@permission_required(\"app.change_userinput\", raise_exception=True)\ndef package_class_view(request):\n    if request.method not in [\"GET\", \"POST\"]:\n        return HttpResponseForbidden(\"Unsupported method\")\n\n    user_input = _get_user_input(request)\n\n    return process_user_input(request, user_input)\n\n\ndef process_user_input(request, user_input):\n    return HttpResponseForbidden(\"Implement authorized business logic here\")\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nDjango\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-community/Broken_Access_Control)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Django Security Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Django_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [Django authentication system](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/auth/default/)\n- [CWE-306: Missing Authentication for Critical Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html)\n- [CWE-862: Missing Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-306",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-862",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-306",
                  "CWE-862"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e1ae6064-ebac-38b2-99de-bd8b8e0969e8",
              "name": "Missing Function Level Access Control",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis is a missing function-level access control issue. The asynchronous view returns an HTML response without enforcing authentication or checking whether the caller is allowed to access the route.\n\nIn Python web applications, a handler like this must not rely on routing structure, hidden URLs, or client behavior for protection. If the endpoint serves internal pages or user-specific content, an unauthenticated or unauthorized request can reach the function directly and receive the response.\n\nFor the shown aiohttp-style sink, add an explicit authentication and authorization check at the start of the handler before loading the template or returning content. Deny access by default, require a verified user object from server-side middleware or session handling, and validate that the user has the required role or permission for this view.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nProtect every sensitive handler with server-side authentication and authorization checks, even if the route is not linked from the UI. In aiohttp applications, enforce this consistently with middleware or decorators that resolve the current user from a trusted session, token, or identity provider and reject requests that lack the required privileges.\n\nUse deny-by-default rules and check specific roles or permissions close to the protected action. Avoid deriving template names or access decisions from untrusted input, and prefer fixed route-to-template mappings for administrative pages. Return 401 for unauthenticated users and 403 for authenticated users without sufficient permission, and log denied access attempts for auditing.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom aiohttp import web\nfrom aiohttp_jinja2 import get_template\n\n\ndef require_authenticated_user(handler):\n    async def wrapped(request):\n        user = request.get(\"user\")\n        if user is None or not user.get(\"is_authenticated\", False):\n            raise web.HTTPUnauthorized(text=\"Authentication required\")\n        return await handler(request)\n    return wrapped\n\n\ndef require_role(required_role):\n    def decorator(handler):\n        async def wrapped(request):\n            user = request.get(\"user\")\n            roles = user.get(\"roles\", []) if user else []\n            if required_role not in roles:\n                raise web.HTTPForbidden(text=\"Forbidden\")\n            return await handler(request)\n        return wrapped\n    return decorator\n\n\n@require_authenticated_user\n@require_role(\"admin\")\nasync def _root_view(request):\n    template = get_template(\"root.html\", request.app)\n    return web.Response(text=template.render(), content_type=\"text/html\")\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\naiohttp\naiohttp-jinja2\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-community/Broken_Access_Control)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-306: Missing Authentication for Critical Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html)\n- [CWE-862: Missing Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html)\n- [aiohttp Advanced Web Server Usage](https://docs.aiohttp.org/en/stable/web_advanced.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-306",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-862",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-306",
                  "CWE-862"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e4e927eb-c261-3472-8803-e184891e5b9a",
              "name": "Insecure YAML Deserialization Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsafe YAML deserialization can occur when Python code parses untrusted YAML with a loader that allows arbitrary object construction. In this case, `yaml.load_all(..., Loader=yaml.UnsafeLoader)` accepts attacker-controlled YAML documents and can instantiate Python objects or trigger behaviors that lead to arbitrary code execution.\n\nFor the sink shown here, the fix is to replace the unsafe loader with PyYAML's safe loader so only standard YAML types are deserialized. If multiple YAML documents are expected, use `yaml.safe_load_all` or `yaml.load_all(..., Loader=yaml.SafeLoader)` and then validate the resulting data structure before using it in application logic.\n\nIf the application truly requires custom object deserialization, do not allow direct parsing of untrusted input with a permissive loader. Instead, restrict accepted schema, map parsed values into explicit Python types, and reject unexpected keys, tags, or nested structures.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nUse `yaml.safe_load` for single-document input and `yaml.safe_load_all` for multi-document input when parsing any YAML that may be influenced by users, files, requests, environment variables, or external systems. Safe loaders deserialize only simple data types such as dictionaries, lists, strings, numbers, booleans, and null.\n\nAfter parsing, validate the resulting data against the expected structure before further processing. Prefer explicit schema validation and reject unknown fields or unexpected nesting. If custom classes are needed, deserialize into plain data first and then construct trusted application objects manually rather than letting the YAML parser instantiate them.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport yaml\n\nresult = list(yaml.safe_load_all(user_input))\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nPyYAML\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Deserialization_of_untrusted_data)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)\n- [PyYAML Documentation](https://pyyaml.org/wiki/PyYAMLDocumentation)\n- [PyYAML unsafe_load Remote Code Execution Overview](https://sourcery.ai/vulnerabilities/python-pyyaml-unsafe-load-rce)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-502",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-502"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e71b572d-95da-3d5f-889e-9cb4b40de582",
              "name": "Insecure Deserialization of Untrusted Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsafe deserialization. In Python, calling pickle.load on data influenced by a user is dangerous because the pickle format can instantiate objects and execute attacker-controlled behavior during deserialization. That turns untrusted input into code execution risk, not just a parsing problem.\n\nIn this sink, return pickle.load(user_input) directly deserializes attacker-controlled content. If an attacker can supply or modify the stream, they can craft a payload that runs arbitrary code when the application loads it. For untrusted data, replace pickle with a safe data format such as JSON and validate the decoded structure before using it.\n\nIf the application must persist Python objects internally, only unpickle data from a trusted source protected by integrity controls, and never accept pickle content from requests, files, or messages that an external party can influence. For this sink, the correct fix is to stop using pickle.load on user input and parse a constrained format instead.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not deserialize untrusted input with pickle, marshal, or shelve. Use a non-executable serialization format such as JSON for data received from users, APIs, message queues, cookies, or files outside the application's trust boundary. After parsing, enforce strict schema validation, expected types, size limits, and allowed fields before the data reaches business logic.\n\nIf legacy code still relies on pickle for internal storage, isolate that usage to trusted data only, protect the serialized blob with authentication and integrity checks, and ensure no external input can reach the deserializer. Prefer explicit object mapping from validated primitives rather than automatic reconstruction of arbitrary Python objects.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport json\nfrom typing import BinaryIO, Any\n\ndef load_user_data(user_input: BinaryIO) -> Any:\n    raw = user_input.read()\n    if isinstance(raw, bytes):\n        raw = raw.decode(\"utf-8\")\n\n    data = json.loads(raw)\n\n    if not isinstance(data, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"Expected a JSON object\")\n\n    allowed_keys = {\"id\", \"name\", \"enabled\"}\n    if not set(data.keys()).issubset(allowed_keys):\n        raise ValueError(\"Unexpected fields in input\")\n\n    if \"id\" in data and not isinstance(data[\"id\"], int):\n        raise ValueError(\"id must be an integer\")\n    if \"name\" in data and not isinstance(data[\"name\"], str):\n        raise ValueError(\"name must be a string\")\n    if \"enabled\" in data and not isinstance(data[\"enabled\"], bool):\n        raise ValueError(\"enabled must be a boolean\")\n\n    return data\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\njson\ntyping\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Deserialization of untrusted data](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Deserialization_of_untrusted_data)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)\n- [Python pickle documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/pickle.html)\n- [Python json documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/json.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-502",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-502"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ecc8bedb-688e-3ac1-b0d9-9d3852d1ac2f",
              "name": "Weak Or Broken Hash Function Used",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nBroken cryptographic hash usage. This code computes an MD5 digest from user-controlled input and converts it to hex. In Python, hashlib.md5 is fast and collision-prone, so it is not appropriate for security-sensitive purposes such as integrity checks, signatures, token derivation, or password handling.\n\nReplace MD5 with a modern primitive based on the actual security goal. For general hashing or integrity-style use, use hashlib.sha256 or stronger. If the value is used to store or verify passwords, do not use a general-purpose hash at all; use a password hashing function such as hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac, bcrypt, scrypt, or Argon2. For the sink shown here, switching to SHA-256 preserves the same hex-encoded output pattern while removing the weak algorithm.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nUse hashlib.sha256, hashlib.sha384, or hashlib.sha512 for non-password security hashing, and compare resulting digests with hmac.compare_digest when verifying expected values. If authenticity is required, use HMAC with a secret key instead of a plain hash. If the data is a password, store a salted password-KDF output such as hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac, scrypt, bcrypt, or Argon2 rather than any fast hash.\n\nAlso ensure the input type is bytes before hashing. If the source value is a string, encode it explicitly with UTF-8 so the behavior is deterministic across callers and runtimes.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom hashlib import sha256\n\ndigest = sha256(user_input).hexdigest()\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nfrom hashlib import sha256\nfrom hmac import compare_digest\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Use_of_a_Broken_or_Risky_Cryptographic_Algorithm)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)\n- [Python hashlib documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/hashlib.html)\n- [OWASP Password Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-327",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-327"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "eeba82c4-dcb8-3f2c-92b8-e7b576328a55",
              "name": "Weak SHA1 Hash For Security",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nWeak cryptographic hash algorithm. In Python, creating a digest with hashlib.new(\"SHA1\") uses SHA-1, which is no longer appropriate for security-sensitive purposes because collision attacks are practical against it.\n\nIf this hash is used for integrity checks, signatures, password handling, token generation, or any security decision, an attacker may be able to exploit SHA-1 weaknesses to bypass trust assumptions. For the sink shown here, replace SHA-1 with a modern hash such as SHA-256 or SHA-512. If the code is only computing a non-security checksum for compatibility or deduplication, explicitly mark that intent with usedforsecurity=False where supported.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nUse SHA-256 or SHA-512 for general-purpose security hashing in Python. For password storage, do not use raw hashes; use a dedicated password hashing function such as bcrypt, scrypt, or Argon2. Reserve SHA-1 only for legacy interoperability that cannot yet be removed, and in that case make the non-security intent explicit with usedforsecurity=False if your Python and OpenSSL build support it. Review all callers to ensure the digest is not used in authentication, signature validation, integrity enforcement, or any other security boundary.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport hashlib\n\nh = hashlib.new(\"sha256\")\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nhashlib (Python standard library)\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)\n- [Python hashlib documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/hashlib.html)\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-327",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-327"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f0e82ca8-9b70-33ca-9cf6-4f51ed8a955c",
              "name": "Vulnerable XML Parsing With Untrusted Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nVulnerability label: unsafe XML parsing of untrusted input.\n\nIn Python, calling xml.sax.parseString on attacker-controlled XML can allow XML-based attacks such as external entity expansion, entity abuse, and denial-of-service conditions caused by hostile payloads. The risk exists because standard XML parsers may process dangerous XML constructs unless they are explicitly hardened.\n\nFor the shown sink, user_input is passed directly into sax.parseString with a ContentHandler. The fix is to replace the standard-library parser call with the hardened equivalent from defusedxml.sax so untrusted XML is parsed with secure protections enabled. If a broader application-wide approach is required, initialize defusedxml.defuse_stdlib() before any XML parsing occurs.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not parse untrusted XML with the standard xml.sax parser. Use defusedxml.sax.parseString for direct replacements of SAX parsing, or call defusedxml.defuse_stdlib() early in process startup to harden standard-library XML modules consistently. Treat all XML from users, APIs, uploads, and message queues as untrusted.\n\nAlso reduce XML attack surface where possible: reject unexpected document types, enforce input size limits before parsing, and avoid enabling features that resolve external entities or fetch remote resources. Keep XML parsing isolated to the minimum functionality needed and prefer safer data formats when XML is not required.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom defusedxml import sax\nfrom xml.sax.handler import ContentHandler\n\ndef parse_user_xml(user_input: str):\n    return sax.parseString(user_input, ContentHandler())\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\npip install defusedxml\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: XML External Entity (XXE) Processing](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/XML_External_Entity_(XXE)_Processing)\n- [CWE-611: Improper Restriction of XML External Entity Reference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html)\n- [defusedxml on PyPI](https://pypi.org/project/defusedxml/)\n- [Python documentation: xml.sax](https://docs.python.org/3/library/xml.sax.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-611",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-611"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f2c2c748-1d70-3571-9c7f-077458523b83",
              "name": "Insecure XML Parsing With Untrusted Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsafe XML parsing of untrusted input. In Python, calling lxml.etree.fromstring on attacker-controlled data can allow XML-based attacks such as external entity expansion, file disclosure, server-side request forgery, or denial of service from malicious payloads.\n\nThe sink shown parses user_input directly with etree.fromstring, so the parser may process dangerous XML constructs before your code can validate the content. For untrusted XML, use a hardened parser designed to block unsafe entity handling and related attacks. Replace the direct parse call with the defusedxml equivalent so untrusted input is rejected safely during parsing.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not parse attacker-controlled XML with the default lxml parsing entry points. Use defusedxml for XML received from requests, message queues, uploaded files, or any other external source. Apply input size limits before parsing, reject unexpected document types, and treat parsing errors as untrusted input failures rather than trying to recover. If XML is not required, prefer simpler data formats such as JSON for externally supplied data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom defusedxml.lxml import fromstring\n\ndef parse_user_xml(user_input):\n    return fromstring(user_input)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\npip install defusedxml\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: XML External Entity (XXE) Processing](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/XML_External_Entity_(XXE)_Processing)\n- [OWASP XML Security Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/XML_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-611: Improper Restriction of XML External Entity Reference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html)\n- [defusedxml on PyPI](https://pypi.org/project/defusedxml/)\n- [lxml.etree API documentation](https://lxml.de/apidoc/lxml.etree.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-611",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-611"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f44ed38d-2b9f-3358-9b44-8416e66ae035",
              "name": "Subprocess Called With Shell=True Can Lead To Command Injection",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis is an operating system command injection risk. In Python, passing a command string into os.system(cmd) causes the shell to interpret the entire string, so any untrusted data embedded in that command can change what actually runs.\n\nIf cmd contains user-controlled input, shell metacharacters such as ;, &&, |, $, backticks, or redirection operators can be used to execute additional commands or alter arguments. That can lead to arbitrary command execution with the privileges of the running process.\n\nReplace os.system with subprocess.run and pass arguments as a list with shell=False so Python invokes the target program directly without shell parsing. Where inputs influence command arguments, validate them against a strict allowlist and reject unexpected values before execution.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not build shell command strings from concatenated input and do not use os.system for dynamic commands. Prefer subprocess.run, subprocess.check_call, or subprocess.check_output with an argument list and the default shell=False behavior. Use absolute executable paths where practical to avoid path manipulation issues.\n\nAccept only expected command arguments, ideally from a fixed allowlist of known values. If a shell is truly unavoidable, keep untrusted data out of the command entirely; quoting alone is not a complete defense when safer process-spawning APIs are available. Also run the process with the least privileges necessary and avoid invoking external commands when a Python standard-library function can perform the same task.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport re\nimport subprocess\n\n_SAFE_ARG = re.compile(r\"^[A-Za-z0-9._/-]+$\")\n\n\ndef run_command(user_arg: str) -> None:\n    if not _SAFE_ARG.fullmatch(user_arg):\n        raise ValueError(\"invalid command argument\")\n\n    subprocess.run([\n        \"/usr/bin/somecommand\",\n        user_arg,\n    ], check=True, shell=False)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nimport re\nimport subprocess\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [OWASP OS Command Injection Defense Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/OS_Command_Injection_Defense_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n- [Python subprocess module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-78",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f45791d9-3686-30da-8159-0abe1f3b671a",
              "name": "Jinja2 Environment Without Autoescape Enabled",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis is an unsafe HTML templating configuration that can lead to cross-site scripting (XSS). In Python, creating a Jinja2 Environment without autoescaping means template expressions such as {{ value }} are rendered as raw HTML instead of being escaped for browser output.\n\nAt the shown sink, environment = Environment(loader=loader), the default behavior leaves HTML escaping disabled unless it is explicitly configured. If any untrusted data is passed into templates rendered by this environment, an attacker can inject markup or script that executes in the browser. The fix is to enable Jinja2 autoescaping when constructing the environment so HTML and XML templates escape untrusted values by default.\n\nFor this sink, use select_autoescape(...) so escaping is automatically applied to HTML-like templates, or set autoescape=True if the environment is dedicated to HTML output only. Keep raw output opt-in and limited to trusted content.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nEnable autoescaping on every Jinja2 Environment that renders browser-facing content. Prefer select_autoescape for mixed template sets so HTML and XML templates are escaped automatically based on filename, and use autoescape=True only when the environment is exclusively used for HTML output.\n\nDo not rely on template authors to remember manual escaping. Treat all variables passed into templates as untrusted by default, and only mark content safe when it has been intentionally sanitized or generated from trusted sources. Review uses of the safe filter, Markup, and custom filters to ensure they do not reintroduce raw HTML rendering of attacker-controlled data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom jinja2 import Environment, FileSystemLoader, select_autoescape\n\nloader = FileSystemLoader(\"templates\")\nenvironment = Environment(\n    loader=loader,\n    autoescape=select_autoescape(\n        enabled_extensions=(\"html\", \"htm\", \"xml\"),\n        default_for_string=True,\n        default=False,\n    ),\n)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\njinja2\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [Jinja2 Environment API](https://jinja.palletsprojects.com/en/stable/api/#jinja2.Environment)\n- [Jinja2 select_autoescape](https://jinja.palletsprojects.com/en/stable/api/#jinja2.select_autoescape)\n- [OWASP Cross Site Scripting (XSS)](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/xss/)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f87ddd31-3772-367a-b44a-dbebfeecd741",
              "name": "Missing Function Level Access Control",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThis issue is missing function-level access control. In Python web applications, a route handler that is exposed without authentication or authorization checks can be reached by any client that can access the endpoint, which allows unauthenticated users to trigger application behavior that should be restricted.\n\nIn the shown aiohttp route, the GET handler for \"/\" performs a redirect without verifying the caller identity or permissions first. If this endpoint is intended only for authenticated users, the handler should enforce authentication before returning the redirect and should deny access when the user is not logged in or lacks the required role. Apply the access check at the route entry point so every request is validated consistently.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nRequire authentication and, where applicable, role or permission checks before executing every protected route. In aiohttp, enforce this with middleware or a decorator so handlers cannot be exposed accidentally. Return 401 for unauthenticated requests and 403 for authenticated users who lack permission. Do not rely on client-side navigation, hidden links, or obscurity of URLs as access control. Review redirect targets and downstream endpoints as well, because protecting only the landing route is insufficient if the redirected resource remains publicly accessible.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom aiohttp import web\nfrom aiohttp_session import get_session\n\nroutes = web.RouteTableDef()\n\n\ndef require_authenticated(handler):\n    async def wrapped(request):\n        session = await get_session(request)\n        user_id = session.get(\"user_id\")\n        if not user_id:\n            raise web.HTTPUnauthorized(text=\"Authentication required\")\n        return await handler(request)\n    return wrapped\n\n\n@routes.get(\"/\")\n@require_authenticated\nasync def index(request):\n    raise web.HTTPFound(\"/vulnpy\")\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\naiohttp-session\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-community/Broken_Access_Control)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-306: Missing Authentication for Critical Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html)\n- [aiohttp Web Server Quickstart](https://docs.aiohttp.org/en/stable/web_quickstart.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-306",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-306"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fb71f94a-6752-3d13-8ebb-d15edcf086a7",
              "name": "Subprocess Called With Shell=True Can Lead To Command Injection",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nOS command injection. In Python, passing a command string into shell-executing APIs such as os.system allows shell metacharacters, chained commands, redirection, and variable expansion to be interpreted by the operating system. If any part of the command comes from untrusted input, an attacker can execute unintended commands with the privileges of the running process.\n\nFor the sink shown, the fix is to stop invoking a shell and execute the intended program directly with an argument list. In Python, prefer subprocess.run with shell=False and provide each argument as a separate list element. If only a small set of commands or flags is valid, enforce an allowlist before execution so user input cannot change the command structure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nReplace os.system and any subprocess usage with shell=True by direct process execution using subprocess.run or subprocess.Popen with shell=False. Build the command as a list of fixed program and argument values instead of concatenating a single string. Validate untrusted input against a strict allowlist of expected values, especially for command names, subcommands, flags, paths, and filenames. If shell use is absolutely unavoidable, do not pass raw user input into the command string; redesign the interface to avoid shell interpretation and reduce privileges of the executing process.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\n\ndef run_command(user_arg: str) -> int:\n    allowed = {\"start\", \"stop\", \"status\"}\n    if user_arg not in allowed:\n        raise ValueError(\"invalid command argument\")\n\n    completed = subprocess.run(\n        [\"/usr/bin/service\", \"myservice\", user_arg],\n        check=False,\n        shell=False\n    )\n    return completed.returncode\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n- [Python subprocess module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html)\n- [Python shlex documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/shlex.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-78",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fd5efed1-3906-377f-8293-1683f694011e",
              "name": "Starting A Process With A Shell",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCommand injection in Python process execution. Using os.system(cmd) starts a shell and passes the command string to it, so any untrusted data embedded in cmd can be interpreted as shell syntax instead of plain argument data. Attackers can exploit separators, substitutions, redirects, or environment-dependent behavior to execute unintended commands.\n\nIn Python, the safest fix is to avoid shell-based execution for external processes. Replace os.system(cmd) with subprocess.run() and pass arguments as a list with shell=False, which is the default. This prevents the shell from parsing user-controlled input and keeps each argument isolated.\n\nIf the command includes variable input, validate that input against a strict allowlist before invoking the process. When shell features are truly required, redesign the flow to avoid direct user control over the command string; quoting alone is weaker than removing the shell from the execution path.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not use os.system, os.popen, or subprocess APIs with shell=True for commands influenced by external input. Invoke programs directly with subprocess.run or subprocess.Popen using a list of arguments so the shell does not interpret metacharacters. Prefer fixed executable paths and fixed option names, and validate dynamic values with an allowlist of expected characters or known-safe values. If you must support a limited set of actions, map user choices to predefined commands rather than concatenating strings. Handle errors explicitly with check=True or return-code checks, and avoid passing unsanitized data through environment variables, file names, or command options that may alter program behavior.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\n\n# Build the command as an argument list, not a shell string.\n# Validate or constrain any untrusted values before use.\ncommand = [\"/usr/bin/example\", \"--name\", user_value]\nsubprocess.run(command, check=True)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nsubprocess\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n- [Python subprocess module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html)\n- [Python shlex module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/shlex.html)\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-78",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            }
          ],
          "language": "en-US",
